CAT = "iiqeRisk"; SEG = "RSK"
Q = [
("In the IIQE study of insurance, risk is best defined as:",
 "Uncertainty about a possible loss", ["The certainty that a loss will happen", "The premium charged for a policy", "The cause of every insured loss"],
 "Risk is uncertainty about whether a loss of a certain kind will occur, or how much will be lost. That uncertainty is what creates the wish to insure."),
("Which kind of potential loss is NOT normally commercially insurable?",
 "Emotional loss such as grief", ["Financial loss such as theft", "Physical loss such as injury", "Loss of a building by fire"],
 "Financial and physical losses can be insured; grief and sorrow cannot be measured or bought cover for, so emotional loss is not a commercially insurable risk."),
("A risk that offers the chance of loss or no change, but never gain, is a:",
 "Pure risk", ["Speculative risk", "Fundamental risk", "Dynamic risk"],
 "Pure risks such as fire and accident offer loss or, at best, no change. Speculative risks also offer the chance of gain."),
("Buying shares in a new business venture is an example of a:",
 "Speculative risk", ["Pure risk", "Fundamental risk", "Particular risk"],
 "A business venture may make or lose money, so it is speculative. Speculative risks are taken voluntarily for gain and are not normally insured."),
("Why do commercial insurers not normally insure speculative risks?",
 "The insured would lose the will to succeed", ["Speculative losses are always too small to matter", "They affect only one person at a time", "The law bans insurance on any business"],
 "Speculative risks are entered into for gain. If the loss side were insured, the person would have little reason to work for that gain."),
("A war that disrupts trade across several countries is an example of a:",
 "Fundamental risk", ["Particular risk", "Speculative risk", "Pure peril only"],
 "Fundamental risks arise from causes outside any individual's control and affect large numbers of people, such as war, famine or widespread flood."),
("A car accident that injures one driver is an example of a:",
 "Particular risk", ["Fundamental risk", "Speculative risk", "Uninsurable risk"],
 "Particular risks have localised consequences for an individual or a small group, even if they are serious for those involved."),
("Fundamental risks are not normally insurable commercially because:",
 "Losses would be too widespread", ["They never cause financial loss", "They involve the chance of gain", "They are always caused by the insured"],
 "Because fundamental risks hit very many people at once, it is considered financially infeasible for insurers to handle them commercially."),
("When an underwriter refers to 'the risk' in a proposal for a warehouse, the word most likely means:",
 "The property being insured", ["The insurer's profit margin", "The broker's commission", "The policy wording itself"],
 "Practitioners use 'risk' for the property or person being insured, and also for the peril insured against, as well as for uncertainty of loss."),
("A policy written on an 'all risks' basis covers:",
 "Any cause not excluded", ["Fire and theft only", "Only the perils listed by name", "Every loss with no exclusions"],
 "'All risks' cover means any loss due to any cause is covered unless that cause is excluded, so exclusions still apply."),
("As a branch of management, risk management seeks to identify, quantify and then:",
 "Deal with the risks", ["Insure every risk in full", "Transfer all risk to staff", "Ignore risks below a set size"],
 "Risk management identifies risks, measures them and then deals with them, whether by control, transfer or financing. Insurance is only one of its tools."),
("A company drops plans to build a chemical plant so that it never faces a chemical explosion. This is:",
 "Risk avoidance", ["Loss reduction", "Risk transfer", "Self-insurance"],
 "Avoidance removes the chance of a loss by not being exposed to the peril at all, here by abandoning the project."),
("A factory runs regular safety training to cut the number of accidents. This is an example of:",
 "Loss prevention", ["Loss reduction", "Risk avoidance", "Risk financing"],
 "Loss prevention lowers the frequency of losses. Loss reduction lowers their severity once they happen."),
("Fitting an automatic sprinkler system mainly aims at:",
 "Loss reduction", ["Loss prevention", "Risk avoidance", "Risk transfer"],
 "A sprinkler does not stop fires starting; it limits the damage once a fire starts, which lowers the severity of the loss."),
("Shifting the financial effect of a pure risk to another party by contract or insurance is called:",
 "Risk transfer", ["Risk avoidance", "Loss prevention", "Risk retention"],
 "Risk transfer moves a risk of loss from one party to another; buying insurance is the most common form."),
("A bus operator decides to pay minor bodywork repairs to its fleet from a reserve it builds up itself. This is:",
 "Self-insurance", ["Reinsurance", "Loss prevention", "Risk avoidance"],
 "Building an internal fund to pay one's own losses is self-insurance, a risk financing tool alongside insurance and non-insurance transfer."),
("Which statement about insurance and risk management is correct?",
 "Insurance is one tool of risk management", ["Insurance replaces risk management", "Risk management applies to insured risks only", "Insurance prevents all losses"],
 "Insurance is closely linked to risk management but is only one of its tools, alongside avoidance, loss control and other financing methods."),
("A supermarket compares stock counts with cash receipts to measure its losses from theft. Which risk management step is this?",
 "Quantifying the risk", ["Identifying the risk", "Transferring the risk", "Avoiding the risk"],
 "Measuring how much is being lost is quantification; spotting the possible causes (customer or staff theft) was identification."),
("The primary function of insurance is best described as:",
 "Transferring financial loss", ["Providing employment to agents", "Funding government projects", "Collecting savings for banks"],
 "Insurance is essentially a risk transfer mechanism: for a premium, the potential financial loss moves from the individual to the insurer."),
("Insurer surveys followed by recommendations to improve a risk are an ancillary benefit known as:",
 "Loss control", ["Risk avoidance", "Speculation", "Reinsurance"],
 "Surveys, inspections and risk improvement requirements lead to fewer fires and accidents, a secondary benefit called loss prevention and reduction, or loss control."),
("Why does insurance encourage economic development such as building projects?",
 "Investors and lenders need cover", ["Insurers build the projects", "Premiums are paid by the government", "It removes the need for capital"],
 "Few people would put capital into costly projects without insurance, and bank finance is often unavailable without cover, so insurance makes projects possible."),
("Which is an ancillary, rather than primary, benefit of insurance to Hong Kong?",
 "A source of jobs", ["Compensation after an insured loss", "Moving loss from client to insurer", "Payment after a death claim"],
 "Compensation and risk transfer are the primary benefits. Employment, savings, loss control and economic growth are ancillary benefits."),
("Life insurance offers which ancillary benefit to the community?",
 "A way to save for the future", ["Lower motor premiums", "Protection against speculation", "Removal of fundamental risk"],
 "Life insurance gives a convenient and effective means of saving and investing, providing for old age and family needs."),
("Which pair of risk classifications is based on cause and effect?",
 "Particular and fundamental", ["Pure and speculative", "Physical and emotional loss", "Insured and excepted"],
 "Pure and speculative classify risks by their financial result; particular and fundamental classify them by cause and how widely they affect people."),
]
